    <h1>Handle Generation Using FarmHash64</h1>
    <p>Using FarmHash64 for handle generation can be an efficient approach in high-performance applications. Here's a comprehensive guide on how to implement it in a C native library and call it from C# managed code.</p>
    
    <h2>C Code (Native Library)</h2>
    <pre><code>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include "farmhash.h"  // Include the FarmHash library

typedef struct {
    uint64_t hash;
} Handle;

static uint64_t running_count = 0;

Handle CreateHandle() {
    Handle handle;
    handle.hash = FarmHash64((char*)&running_count, sizeof(running_count));
    running_count++;
    return handle;
}

int CompareHandles(Handle* h1, Handle* h2) {
    return h1->hash == h2->hash;
}

void PrintHandle(Handle* handle) {
    printf("%016llx\n", handle->hash);
}

int main() {
    Handle h1 = CreateHandle();
    Handle h2 = CreateHandle();

    PrintHandle(&h1);
    PrintHandle(&h2);

    printf("Comparison: %d\n", CompareHandles(&h1, &h2));

    return 0;
}
    </code></pre>

    <h2>C# Code (Managed Code)</h2>
    <pre><code>
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

public static class NativeMethods
{
    [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]
    public struct Handle
    {
        public ulong hash;
    }

    [DllImport("YourNativeLibrary.dll", CallingConvention = CallingConvention.Cdecl)]
    public static extern Handle CreateHandle();

    [DllImport("YourNativeLibrary.dll", CallingConvention = CallingConvention.Cdecl)]
    public static extern int CompareHandles(ref Handle h1, ref Handle h2);
}

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var h1 = NativeMethods.CreateHandle();
        var h2 = NativeMethods.CreateHandle();

        Console.WriteLine(h1.hash.ToString("X"));
        Console.WriteLine(h2.hash.ToString("X"));

        int comparison = NativeMethods.CompareHandles(ref h1, ref h2);
        Console.WriteLine($"Comparison: {comparison}");
    }
}
    </code></pre>

    <h2>Considerations</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Speed:</strong> FarmHash64 is known for its speed, making it suitable for high-performance applications where quick handle generation is required.</li>
        <li><strong>Simplicity:</strong> The simplicity of FarmHash64 makes it easy to implement and integrate into existing systems.</li>
        <li><strong>Uniform Distribution:</strong> FarmHash64 provides a good uniform distribution of hash values, reducing the likelihood of collisions in a well-sized handle pool.</li>
        <li><strong>Security:</strong> While FarmHash64 is not a cryptographic hash function, it provides good performance for general-purpose hashing. If security is a major concern, especially against collision attacks, consider using a more secure hash function like SHA-256, although it would be slower.</li>
        <li><strong>Collision Handling:</strong> Ensure that your implementation includes robust collision handling mechanisms. Even with a good hash function, the possibility of collisions exists, and your system should handle them gracefully.</li>
    </ul>
